{:edb/db-before-t 1344 :edb/db-after-t 1345 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534657 50 #inst "2026-10-12T06:13:41.775Z" 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186107423 1078 #edb/ref 17592186107426 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186109165 1078 #edb/ref 17592186109167 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186110294 1074 "A straight angle has a degree measure of $180^\\circ$. This corresponds to one-half of a full turn.\n\nAmong the given options, the only straight angle is the following:\n\n![](images/1e/1e16add703d800bce68d70ded10f220808accb557ae15f9245147eaf3ca513b1.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is III." 13194139534657 17592186045417 false] [17592186110294 1074 "A straight angle has a degree measure of $180^\\circ.$ This corresponds to one-half of a full turn.\n\nAmong the given options, the only straight angle is the following:\n\n \n\n![](images/1e/1e16add703d800bce68d70ded10f220808accb557ae15f9245147eaf3ca513b1.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is III." 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186110392 1071 "![](images/86/867b3664e93f42c55a2f97dc28bf66794484e226981632c85f725926cec77def.png)\n\nWhat type of angle is $\\angle RST$, shown in the figure above?" 13194139534657 17592186045417 false] [17592186110392 1071 "![](images/86/867b3664e93f42c55a2f97dc28bf66794484e226981632c85f725926cec77def.png)\n\nWhat type of angle is $\\angle RST,$ shown in the figure above?" 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186110392 1074 "Notice that the vertex of $\\angle RST$ is point $S$, not point $T$. So $\\angle RST$ is *not* a straight angle.\n\nAs a result, the two rays $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{SR}$ and $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{ST}$ of our angle coincide. So, we can think of $\\angle RST$ as:\n\n- A *null* angle, assuming it doesn't turn at all.\n\n![](images/a8/a8916a724d4463bf2047c919b73770160d3124838b61af0dcdc2b9e04322381a.png)\n\n- A *full* angle, assuming it makes one full turn.\n\n![](images/9c/9cd768b4361e95a18ffa52d6315fb5ea6cd0cf6784bc4c9e12b1bae1f6566755.png)\n\nSo, the answer is \"Null\" or \"Full.\"" 13194139534657 17592186045417 false] [17592186110392 1074 "Notice that the vertex of $\\angle RST$ is point $S,$ not point $T.$ So $\\angle RST$ is *not* a straight angle.\n\nAs a result, the two rays $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{SR}$ and $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{ST}$ of our angle coincide. So, we can think of $\\angle RST$ as:\n\n- A *null* angle, assuming it doesn't turn at all.\n\n![](images/a8/a8916a724d4463bf2047c919b73770160d3124838b61af0dcdc2b9e04322381a.png)\n\n- A *full* angle, assuming it makes one full turn.\n\n![](images/9c/9cd768b4361e95a18ffa52d6315fb5ea6cd0cf6784bc4c9e12b1bae1f6566755.png)\n\nSo, the answer is \"Null\" or \"Full.\"" 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186110672 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431268 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186110672 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431269 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186110672 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431271 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186110672 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431272 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186110673 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431273 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186110673 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431275 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186110673 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431276 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186110673 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431278 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186110673 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431279 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186110763 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431267 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186110763 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431270 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186110763 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431274 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186110763 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431277 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186110864 1050 420.0 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186110922 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186110922 1074 "A right angle has a degree measure of ${90}^{∘}.$ This corresponds to one-quarter of a full turn.\n\nAmong the given options, the only right angle is the following:\n\n![](images/bc/bc5cc4627ba0cb06a7f38ccdfc1621dcdf55d431d637b43fbf5c5ad8c9229acd.png)" 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186111240 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186111240 1074 "A full angle has a degree measure of ${360}^{∘}.$ This corresponds to a full turn.\n\nAmong the given options, the only full angle is the following:\n\n![](images/a5/a52ce1848c072141aa67b53b27546d1d725ed66b28254bd0f1a0eb29a27bc3f5.png)" 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186111837 1064 "Some angles have special names.\n\n- A **right angle** has a measure of $90^\\circ$. It corresponds to one-quarter of a full turn.![](images/15/155a1d6d2abd868b05674dec47ef4b20a5147d30cd328a5ba53d00dea271d65c.png)\n- A **straight angle**, which is just a straight line, has a measure of $180^\\circ$. It corresponds to one-half of a full turn.![](images/24/2423950f80de8354aee74c4f1daa6e42cf376caa0365307c76ebc7e09f96c8cf.png)\n\n- A **null angle** has a measure of $0^\\circ$. You can imagine \"closing\" the angle until the two sides of the angle overlap. In this case, we don't have any turn, so its measure is zero!![](images/f8/f86b7c1b7ba758fb6b07b6aeabd03395d29103b45e4cd220444b675f70a6e630.png)\n- A **full angle** has a measure of $360^\\circ$. You can imagine \"opening\" the angle until one of the sides goes all the way around. So, this is the angle between a ray and itself after it has made one full turn.![](images/cb/cbc9e288f4d3905357539a221954328ef0ac799531fab62f034364d32a7c0f29.png)\n\nOn a diagram, null and full angles can look the same, so it is up to us what we choose to call it." 13194139534657 17592186045417 false] [17592186111837 1064 "Some angles have special names.\n\n- A **right angle** has a measure of $90^\\circ.$ It corresponds to one-quarter of a full turn.\n\n![](images/15/155a1d6d2abd868b05674dec47ef4b20a5147d30cd328a5ba53d00dea271d65c.png)\n\n- A **straight angle**, which is just a straight line, has a measure of $180^\\circ.$ It corresponds to one-half of a full turn.\n\n![](images/24/2423950f80de8354aee74c4f1daa6e42cf376caa0365307c76ebc7e09f96c8cf.png)\n\n- A **null angle** has a measure of $0^\\circ.$ You can imagine \"closing\" the angle until the two sides of the angle overlap. In this case, we don't have any turn, so its measure is zero!\n\n![](images/f8/f86b7c1b7ba758fb6b07b6aeabd03395d29103b45e4cd220444b675f70a6e630.png)\n\n- A **full angle** has a measure of $360^\\circ.$ You can imagine \"opening\" the angle until one of the sides goes all the way around. So, this is the angle between a ray and itself after it has made one full turn.\n\n![](images/cb/cbc9e288f4d3905357539a221954328ef0ac799531fab62f034364d32a7c0f29.png)\n\nOn a diagram, null and full angles can look the same, so it is up to us what we choose to call it." 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186431189 1080 "Full or Straight" 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186431189 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186431189 1083 #uuid "00626d6e-47ea-5b73-ace3-c239ab797dda" 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186431190 1080 "images/a3/a38e421522cda08a156c3423a623290dc7c6a4db165c3c7a243b85d4f462eae9.png" 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186431190 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186431190 1083 #uuid "02ad844e-8c87-5c5d-b549-5103fa7c31de" 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186431191 1080 "images/fc/fc1014f6b5cb3dca17255a3be05c65491141c4c017ca48be5136b6be7b1b2095.png" 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186431191 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186431191 1083 #uuid "091434ba-eb8d-5e07-a0f7-d8455360e6e8" 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186431192 1080 "images/7a/7ac53ac9bc07fa744da0b0bc6499d1ebd7cfb0be92060f98003cce09060ad